---
import { SitePage } from '@boombapbot/editor';
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';

const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL;
---

<Base
  title="Stay Stoked, Stay Woke · boombapbot"
  description="A footnote to a drum machine made by a white guy in tech, about whose music made drum machines matter."
>
  <SitePage client:load baseUrl={base} current="manifesto" layout="note">
    <h1>Stay Stoked, Stay Woke</h1>
    <p>
      Boom bap is Black music, a kind of ’90s East Coast hip-hop associated with artists like Pete Rock, KRS-One, and
      Wu-Tang Clan. Drum machines were widely used in boom bap. In general, Black artists helped make the drum machine
      one of the defining instruments of modern music. Afrika Bambaataa, Frankie Knuckles, and Juan Atkins are all
      Black artists who used drum machines to create new genres. J Dilla’s beats still inspire beatmakers everywhere.
    </p>
    <p>
      I’m a white guy who works in tech and loves beats. I’ve added a small paid tier to this app and feel this needs
      to be said. White folks have been appropriating and profiting from Black culture for decades. And so much of the
      Black culture we love (jazz, hip-hop, etc.) has a historical context of institutional racism. From chattel
      slavery and Jim Crow to the fight for civil rights, from redlining and gentrification to the murder of George
      Floyd, Black people have had to fight just to exist as humans with human rights.
    </p>
    <p>
      I don’t have all the answers to these problems, but a good start is just honesty. From slang to TikTok dances to
      hip-hop, white people are still appropriating Black culture. The least we can do is stay on the hook. Hopefully,
      we can do better and start dismantling institutional racism.
    </p>
  </SitePage>
</Base>
